# DESCRIPTION
# Perform a hypothesis test for proportions based on the fraction of family-friendly
# roller coasters.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Hypothesis tests)
## DBsection(One sample proportion)
## KEYWORDS('sample proportion', 'hypothesis test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",         'PGML.pl',
    'PGstatisticsmacros.pl', "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

do {
    $pct  = random(70, 85, 5);
    $num  = random(40, 60, 5);
    $s    = int($pct / 100 * $num) + non_zero_random(-5, 5);
    $phat = $s / $num;
    $p0   = $pct / 100;

    $zscore    = abs($phat - $p0) / sqrt($p0 * (1 - $p0) / $num);
    $pval      = 2 * normal_prob($zscore, 'inf');
    $sig_level = list_random(0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.20);
} until $pct / 100 != $phat && abs($pval - $sig_level) < 0.05;

@opts   = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$popup1 = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$popup2 = DropDown([@opts], 3, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$popup3 = DropDown([ "Left-tailed", "Right-tailed", "Two-tailed" ], 2);
$popup4 = DropDown(
    [
        "We will reject the null hypothesis.",
        "We fail to reject the null hypothesis."
    ],
    $pval < $sig_level ? 0 : 1
);

$grade = random(1, 5);

BEGIN_PGML

A teacher believes that [$pct]% of students in grade [$grade] will want to go on a field trip to the local zoo. She performs a hypothesis test to determine if the percentage is the same or different from [$pct]%. The teacher samples [$num] students from grade [$grade] and [$s] reply that they would want to go to the zoo. For the hypothesis test, use a [$sig_level*100]% level of significance.

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: p [_]{$popup1} [____________]{Real($p0)}

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  p [_]{$popup2}   [____________]{Real($pct/100)}

c) What type of test is being asked? [_]{$popup3}

d) What is the alpha level? [`\alpha`] = [__]{Real($sig_level)}{10}

e) What is [`n`]? [_]{Real($num)}{10}

f) What is [`\hat{p}`] (to two decimal places)? [____________]{Real($s/$num)}

g) If the [`p`]-value is [@ Round($pval,3) @], what should be our conclusion? [_]{$popup4}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Since we are testing whether or not [$pct]% of all grade [$grade] students want to go on the field trip, the null hypothesis would be [` H_0: \; p = [$p0] `].
b) In the problem, the teacher wants to test if the number is different from [$pct]%, so the alternative is not equal or [`H_a: \; p \neq [$p0] `]. 
c) Since in the alternative hypothesis is not equal, this is a *two-tailed test*. 
d) The [`\alpha`]-level is the level of significance as a fraction or [$sig_level]. 
e) The value of [`n`] is the number of samples or in this case [$num].
f) The sample proportion [`\hat{p}`] is the fraction of "successes" or in this case, the number students in the sample who want to go to the zoo or [`[$s]/[$num] = [@ Round($s/$num,2) @]`].
g) Recall that we reject the null hypothesis only when the [`p`]-value is less than [`\alpha`] and in this case it [@ $pval < $sig_level ? 'is' : 'is not' @], therefore:  [$popup4] 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
